\section{Overview}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Objectives and overview}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item This material is based on Chapter 8 of the textbook ``A First Course in Design and Analysis of Experiments" \citep{oehlert2000}.
    \item Module outcomes include:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item You will understand complete and incomplete factorial treatment structures.
      \item You will develop an intuitive and formal understanding of interaction effects.
      \item You will be able to specify and fit additive and non-additive two-way ANOVA models.
      \item You will learn how to construct and interpret main effect contrasts and interaction contrasts.
      \item You will know how to fit and analyze factorial designs in R using \texttt{aov()} and \texttt{emmeans()}.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Factorial Treatment Structure}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Introduction to Factorial Structures}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item In many experiments, we want to evaluate the effect of two or more explanatory factors simultaneously.
    \item A \alert{factorial treatment structure} defines treatments as combinations of levels of each factor.
    \item Suppose Factor A has $a$ levels and Factor B has $b$ levels.
    \item A \alert{complete factorial design} includes all $g = a \times b$ possible treatment combinations.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak

  \begin{block}{Example 1: $2 \times 3$ Diet Experiment}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Factor 1: Protein Source (Beef, Cereal, Pork) $\implies b = 3$.
      \item Factor 2: Protein Intake (High, Low) $\implies a = 2$.
      \item Total treatment combinations: $g = 2 \times 3 = 6$ unique diets.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}

  \framebreak

  \begin{block}{Example 2: $2 \times 2$ Fertilizer Experiment}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Factor A: Superphosphate (P) $\rightarrow$ Levels: \{Not Added, Added\}
      \item Factor B: Potash (K) $\rightarrow$ Levels: \{Not Added, Added\}
      \item $g = 2 \times 2 = 4$ treatments:
      \begin{enumerate}
        \item None ($P_1 K_1$)
        \item Superphosphate only ($P_2 K_1$)
        \item Potash only ($P_1 K_2$)
        \item Both Superphosphate and Potash ($P_2 K_2$)
      \end{enumerate}
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}

  \mode<article>{
    Notice the power of a factorial design: instead of running two separate experiments (one for Superphosphate and one for Potash), we run a single experiment that evaluates both factors simultaneously using the exact same experimental units. This yields higher statistical efficiency and allows us to study whether the two factors work together or interfere with each other.
  }
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Main Effects and Interactions}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item We are generally interested in both the \emph{main effects} of each treatment, but also the \emph{interactions} between treatments. Consider the example below (Example 2):
  \end{itemize}
  
  \vspace{2mm}

  \mode<presentation>{
    \begin{table}
      \centering
      \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
      \begin{tabular}{l | c c c | c}
        & \textbf{Beef} & \textbf{Cereal} & \textbf{Pork} & \textbf{Marginal Row Mean} \\
        \hline
        \textbf{High Intake} & $\mu_{11}$ & $\mu_{12}$ & $\mu_{13}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{1\bullet}$ \\
        \textbf{Low Intake}  & $\mu_{21}$ & $\mu_{22}$ & $\mu_{23}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{2\bullet}$ \\
        \hline
        \textbf{Marginal Col Mean}   & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 1}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 2}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 3}$ & $\mu$ (Grand Mean) \\
      \end{tabular}
    \end{table}
  }

  \mode<article>{
    \begin{table}
      \centering
      \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
      \begin{tabular}{l | c c c | c}
        & \textbf{Beef ($j=1$)} & \textbf{Cereal ($j=2$)} & \textbf{Pork ($j=3$)} & \textbf{Marginal Row Mean} \\
        \hline
        \textbf{High Intake ($i=1$)} & $\mu_{11}$ & $\mu_{12}$ & $\mu_{13}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{1\bullet}$ \\
        \textbf{Low Intake ($i=2$)}  & $\mu_{21}$ & $\mu_{22}$ & $\mu_{23}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{2\bullet}$ \\
        \hline
        \textbf{Marginal Col Mean}   & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 1}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 2}$ & $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 3}$ & $\mu$ (Grand Mean) \\
      \end{tabular}
    \end{table}
  }

  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Main Effects:} Evaluated by comparing the marginal row means ($\bar{\mu}_{1\bullet}$ vs. $\bar{\mu}_{2\bullet}$) or the marginal column means ($\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 1}$, $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 2}$, $\bar{\mu}_{\bullet 3}$). For example, what is the difference in response between high and low intake treatments?
    \item \textbf{Interactions:} Evaluated by looking at differences between cells. For example, is the difference between High and Low intake the same for Beef as it is for Cereal?
    $$(\mu_{11} - \mu_{21}) \stackrel{?}{=} (\mu_{12} - \mu_{22})$$
    \item \textbf{The ANOVA Alternative:} While we \emph{could} formalize and test all these specific comparisons using individual contrasts (which we discussed in previous modules), doing so for every combination is tedious and inflates our error rate.
    \item Instead, we will introduce the \alert{Two-Way ANOVA} as an efficient way to test for overall main effects and interactions simultaneously.
    \item Like one-way ANOVA, we will express the total sum of squares in terms of treatments and errors.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \mode<article>{
  If we denote $A$ as the first treatment type, $B$ as the second,  and $AB$ as the interaction, then we will have a similar break down: 
    $$
    SS_T = SS_A + SS_B + SS_{AB} + SS_E
    $$
  }
  
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Notation for Two-Way Factorials}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item To identify observations in a two-way design, we use three subscripts:
    $$y_{ijk}$$
    \begin{itemize}
      \item $i = 1, \dots, a$: level of Factor A
      \item $j = 1, \dots, b$: level of Factor B
      \item $k = 1, \dots, n$: replication number within treatment combination $(i, j)$
    \end{itemize}

    \item Summary Notation:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Sample mean for treatment $(i, j)$: $\bar{y}_{ij\bullet} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n y_{ijk}$
      \item Marginal mean for level $i$ of Factor A: $\bar{y}_{i\bullet\bullet} = \frac{1}{bn} \sum_{j=1}^b \sum_{k=1}^n y_{ijk}$
      \item Marginal mean for level $j$ of Factor B: $\bar{y}_{\bullet j\bullet} = \frac{1}{an} \sum_{i=1}^a \sum_{k=1}^n y_{ijk}$
      \item Overall grand mean: $\bar{y}_{\bullet\bullet\bullet} = \frac{1}{abn} \sum_{i=1}^a \sum_{j=1}^b \sum_{k=1}^n y_{ijk}$
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{The Two-Way ANOVA Model}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Model Specification}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item For completely randomized designs, we will start with \alert{balanced factorial} treatments. All $n_{ij} = n$ for all $i, j$.
    \item We model the response $y_{ijk}$ as:
    $$y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + \epsilon_{ijk}$$
    where:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item $\mu$: Overall grand mean.
      \item $\alpha_i$: Main effect of level $i$ of Factor A.
      \item $\beta_j$: Main effect of level $j$ of Factor B.
      \item $(\alpha\beta)_{ij}$: Interaction effect between level $i$ of A and level $j$ of B.
      \item $\epsilon_{ijk} \stackrel{iid}{\sim} N(0, \sigma^2)$: Random errors.
    \end{itemize}

    \framebreak

    \item \alert{Parameter Identification Restrictions (Sum-to-Zero Constraints):}
    $$
    \sum_{i=1}^a \alpha_i = \sum_{j=1}^b \beta_j = \sum_{i=1}^a (\alpha\beta)_{ik} = \sum_{j=1}^b (\alpha\beta)_{kj} = 0 \text{ for all } k
    $$

    \item \alert{Hierarchy of Models}:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item \textbf{Full (Non-additive) Model}: $y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + \epsilon_{ijk}$
      \item \textbf{Additive Model}: $y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + \epsilon_{ijk}$
      \item \textbf{Main Effect Models}: $y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \epsilon_{ijk}$, \hspace{2mm} $y_{ijk} = \mu + \beta_j + \epsilon_{ijk}$ 
      \item \textbf{Constant Model}: $y_{ijk} = \mu + \epsilon_{ijk}$
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Degrees of Freedom}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \mode<article>{When computing the degrees of freedom for each parameter, note that the complete factorial structure performs and ANOVA for treatment A and B simultaneously.}
    The degrees of freedom for each main effect is computed as we would in a one-way ANOVA:
      \begin{itemize}
        \item Main effect $A$: $(a - 1)$ \mode<article>{where $a$ is the number of treatment levels for factor $A$.}
        \item Main effect $B$: $(b - 1)$ \mode<article>{where $b$ is the number of treatment levels for factor $A$.}
        \item Interaction effect ($AB$): $(a - 1)(b - 1)$.
        
        \mode<article>{
          One way to think about this last degree of freedom calculation is to see how many degrees of freedom are unused if we just treated it as a one-way ANOVA for the individual treatment groups $\mu_{ij}$: There are $ab$ total treatment means and we lose one degree of freedom for the grand mean, so we have $ab - 1$ total degrees of freedom that must be accounted for.
          If there are $(a - 1)$ for the main effects of factor $A$, and $(b - 1)$ for the main effects of factor $B$, then there must be:
          $$
          (ab - 1) - (a - 1) - (b - 1) = ab -a - b + 1 = (a - 1)(b - 1)
          $$
          degrees of freedom left for the interaction effects. 
        }
        
      \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Understanding Interaction Effects}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{What is an Interaction Effect?}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \alert{Definition:} An interaction effect occurs when the effect of one factor on the response depends on the level of another factor.
    \item Ask yourself: \emph{"Does the difference between levels of Factor A stay the same regardless of Factor B?"}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item If \textbf{YES}: The factors are \alert{additive} (No Interaction).
      \item If \textbf{NO}: There is an \alert{interaction} between factors.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}

  \framebreak

  \begin{block}{Intuition: "Difference of Differences"}
    Consider a $2 \times 2$ design with population means $\mu_{ij}$:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Simple main effect of A when Factor B is at level 1: $\mu_{11} - \mu_{21}$
      \item Simple main effect of A when Factor B is at level 2: $\mu_{12} - \mu_{22}$
    \end{itemize}
    The \alert{interaction effect} is the difference between these two simple effects:
    $$(\mu_{11} - \mu_{21}) - (\mu_{12} - \mu_{22})$$
    If this difference equals zero, the effect of A is constant across levels of B. If non-zero, an interaction exists.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile, allowframebreaks]{Graphical Diagnosis: Interaction Plots}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item An \alert{interaction plot} displays treatment group means across levels of one factor, with lines connecting levels of the second factor.
    \item \textbf{Parallel lines} $\implies$ No Interaction (Additive).
    \item \textbf{Non-parallel or crossing lines} $\implies$ Interaction present.
  \end{itemize}

<<interaction_plot_demo, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, echo=FALSE, eval=TRUE>>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
# Additive Scenario
means_add <- matrix(c(10, 15, 20, 25), nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE)
interaction.plot(x.factor = gl(2, 2, labels = c("A1", "A2")),
                 trace.factor = gl(2, 1, 4, labels = c("B1", "B2")),
                 response = c(10, 20, 15, 25),
                 type = "b", pch = c(1, 19), col = c("blue", "red"),
                 legend = TRUE, main = "Additive (No Interaction)",
                 xlab = "Factor A", ylab = "Mean Response",
                 trace.label = 'Factor B')

# Interaction Scenario
interaction.plot(x.factor = gl(2, 2, labels = c("A1", "A2")),
                 trace.factor = gl(2, 1, 4, labels = c("B1", "B2")),
                 response = c(10, 25, 20, 15),
                 type = "b", pch = c(1, 19), col = c("blue", "red"),
                 legend = TRUE, main = "Interaction Present",
                 xlab = "Factor A", ylab = "Mean Response", 
                 trace.label = "Factor B")
par(mfrow = c(1, 1))
@

\end{frame}

\section{ANOVA Mechanics and Hypothesis Testing}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Two-Way ANOVA Table (Balanced Design)}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item For a balanced design ($n$ observations per cell, $N = abn$ total):
  \end{itemize}

\mode<presentation>{
  \begin{table}
  \centering
  \small
  \begin{tabular}{l c l c c}
    \hline
    \textbf{Source} & \textbf{DF} & \textbf{SS} & \textbf{MS} & \textbf{F-Statistic} \\
    \hline
    \textbf{Factor A} & $a - 1$ & $SS_A$ & $MS_A$ & $F_A = \frac{MS_A}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Factor B} & $b - 1$ & $SS_B$ & $MS_B$ & $F_B = \frac{MS_B}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Interaction} & $(a-1)(b-1)$ & $SS_{AB}$ & $MS_{AB}$ & $F_{AB} = \frac{MS_{AB}}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Error} & $ab(n - 1)$ & $SS_E$ & $MS_E$ & \\
    \hline
    \textbf{Total} & $abn - 1$ & $SS_T$ & & \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \end{table}
  
  See compiled notes for detailed formulas.
}

\mode<article>{
  \begin{table}
  \centering
  \small
  \begin{tabular}{l c l c c}
    \hline
    \textbf{Source} & \textbf{DF} & \textbf{Sum of Squares (SS)} & \textbf{Mean Square} & \textbf{F-Statistic} \\
    \hline
    \textbf{Factor A} & $a - 1$ & $SS_A = bn \sum \hat{\alpha}_i^2$ & $MS_A = \frac{SS_A}{a-1}$ & $F_A = \frac{MS_A}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Factor B} & $b - 1$ & $SS_B = an \sum \hat{\beta}_j^2$ & $MS_B = \frac{SS_B}{b-1}$ & $F_B = \frac{MS_B}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Interaction AB} & $(a-1)(b-1)$ & $SS_{AB} = n \sum \sum \hat{\alpha\beta}_{ij}^2$ & $MS_{AB} = \frac{SS_{AB}}{(a-1)(b-1)}$ & $F_{AB} = \frac{MS_{AB}}{MS_E}$ \\
    \textbf{Error} & $ab(n - 1)$ & $SS_E = \sum \sum \sum (y_{ijk} - \bar{y}_{ij\bullet})^2$ & $MS_E = \frac{SS_E}{ab(n-1)}$ & \\
    \hline
    \textbf{Total} & $abn - 1$ & $SS_T = SS_A + SS_B + SS_{AB} + SS_E$ & & \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \end{table}
}


  \framebreak

  \begin{block}{Sequence of Testing: Top-Down Rule}
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item \alert{\textbf{Step 1: Test Interaction ($H_0: (\alpha\beta)_{ij} = 0$ for all $i,j$)}}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item If \textbf{Significant}: Stop! The model is non-additive. Do not interpret main effect $F$-tests in isolation. Examine simple main effects or interaction contrasts.
        \item If \textbf{Not Significant}: Drop interaction term to obtain the additive model.
      \end{itemize}
      \item \alert{\textbf{Step 2: Test Main Effects}}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item If interaction is non-significant, proceed to test $H_0: \alpha_i = 0$ and $H_0: \beta_j = 0$ independently using the main effect $F$-tests.
      \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

% \section{Applied Example in R}
% 
% \begin{frame}[fragile, allowframebreaks]{Dataset: Fertilizer Yields ($2 \times 2$ Factorial)}
%   \begin{itemize}
%     \item Let's simulate and analyze the Superphosphate (P) and Potash (K) experiment ($n=4$ replications per treatment).
%   \end{itemize}

<<fertilizer_data, size='small',eval=TRUE,echo=FALSE>>=
# Create factorial dataset
fert_data <- data.frame(
  P = factor(rep(c("No_P", "P"), each = 8)),
  K = factor(rep(rep(c("No_K", "K"), each = 4), 2)),
  yield = c(183, 176, 291, 254,   # None
            224, 258, 244, 214,   # K only
            356, 300, 301, 271,   # P only
            329, 283, 308, 326)    # P and K
)

# Display sample means across cell combinations
# with(fert_data, tapply(yield, list(P, K), mean))
@

% \framebreak

<<fertilizer_plot, fig.height=4, fig.width=6, fig.align='center', eval=FALSE,echo=FALSE>>=
# Generate interaction plot
interaction.plot(x.factor = fert_data$P, trace.factor = fert_data$K,
                 response = fert_data$yield, fun = mean,
                 type = "b", pch = c(16, 17), col = c("darkred", "darkblue"),
                 xlab = "Superphosphate (P)", ylab = "Mean Yield",
                 trace.label = "Potash (K)")
@

% \framebreak

<<fertilizer_anova, size='small', eval=FALSE,echo=FALSE>>=
# Fit Two-Way ANOVA Model with Interaction
fert_mod <- aov(yield ~ P * K, data = fert_data)
summary(fert_mod)
@

% \mode<article>{
%   Notice the output: The interaction term `P:K` has a high p-value ($p = 0.493$). This indicates there is no statistically significant interaction between Superphosphate and Potash. Consequently, we can safely reduce to an additive model or evaluate main effects directly.
% }
% 
